    <h2 class="article-title">Of Love for God and Neighbor</h2>
    <div class="article-body">
        <p><span data-span="s1" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We love</strong> the Lord our God with all our heart, and with all our soul, and with all our mind.</span> <span data-span="s2" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We love</strong> our neighbor as ourselves.</span> <span data-span="s3" data-article="A14" data-type="direct" data-named="false">Jesus called these the first and great commandment and the second like unto it, and said, "On these two commandments hang all the law and the prophets."</span> <span data-span="s4" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false">When a lawyer pressed Him, "And who is my neighbour?" He told of a Samaritan, a man outside the lawyer's own faith, who stopped and showed mercy on a wounded stranger.</span> <span data-span="s5" data-article="A14" data-type="concept" data-named="false">We are called to be neighbors to all, not only to those who share our faith.</span></p>
        <p><span data-span="s6" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We do</strong> unto others as we would have them do unto us, for this is the law and the prophets.</span> <span data-span="s7" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We love</strong> our enemies, bless those who curse us, pray for those who persecute us, and overcome evil with good.</span> <span data-span="s8" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We forgive</strong> one another, even as God for Christ's sake has forgiven us, and we ask His forgiveness as we forgive others.</span> <span data-span="s9" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We put away</strong> lying and speak truth with our neighbor.</span></p>
        <p><span data-span="s10" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We feed</strong> the hungry, welcome the stranger, clothe the naked, and visit the sick and those in prison, for what we do for the least of these, we do for Him.</span> <span data-span="s11" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We do good</strong> unto all, especially unto the household of faith.</span> <span data-span="s12" data-article="A14" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We offer</strong> hospitality without grudging, remembering that some have entertained angels unawares.</span> <span data-span="s13" data-article="A14" data-type="direct" data-named="false"><strong>We pray</strong> for the sick in faith, for "the prayer of faith shall save the sick, and the Lord shall raise him up," and we pray as Christ prayed, "Thy will be done."</span> <span data-span="s14" data-article="A14" data-type="direct" data-named="false">Jesus gave His disciples a new commandment, "That ye love one another; as I have loved you," and said, "By this shall all men know that ye are my disciples, if ye have love one to another."</span></p>
        <div class="article-rjw">
            <p class="rjw-prompt">Christ commands us to love our neighbors as ourselves. Do you count yourself among those whom God loves? How might recognizing His love for you help you better love yourself and even your most difficult neighbor?</p>
